{:edb/db-before-t 1243 :edb/db-after-t 1244 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534556 50 #inst "2026-10-08T00:50:33.112Z" 13194139534556 true] [17592186068768 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295195 13194139534556 true] [17592186068768 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295230 13194139534556 true] [17592186068769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295237 13194139534556 true] [17592186068769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295244 13194139534556 true] [17592186068770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295202 13194139534556 true] [17592186068770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295209 13194139534556 true] [17592186068770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295216 13194139534556 true] [17592186068772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295188 13194139534556 true] [17592186068772 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295223 13194139534556 true] [17592186068987 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534556 true] [17592186068987 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534556 false] [17592186068987 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y^{\\prime } = y^{2} + 1$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186068987 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+1.$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186068987 1145 "Separate the variables and integrate:\n$$\\frac{dy}{1+y^2}=dx,\\qquad \\arctan y=x+C.$$\nThus $y=\\tan(x+C)$ on any interval between consecutive poles. Differentiating gives $y'=\\sec^2(x+C)=1+y^2$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186068987 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y)=x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069003 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534556 true] [17592186069003 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534556 false] [17592186069003 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069003 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x}{e^{y}}$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186069003 1145 "Multiply by $e^y$ and integrate:\n$$e^y\\frac{dy}{dx}=2x,\\qquad \\int e^y\\,dy=\\int2x\\,dx.$$\nThus $e^y=x^2+C$. Equivalently, $y=\\ln(x^2+C)$ on an interval where $x^2+C>0$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186069003 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}={x}^{2}+C.\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069009 1100 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation:\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x + y}$" 13194139534556 false] [17592186069009 1100 "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069009 1145 "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n{e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069009 1145 "Use $e^{x+y}=e^xe^y$ and multiply both sides by $e^{-y}$:\n$$e^{-y}\\frac{dy}{dx}=e^x.$$\nThe left-hand factor depends only on $y$, and the right-hand side depends only on $x$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186069459 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y^{\\prime } - \\sqrt{y} = 2x\\sqrt{y}$ for $y \\ne 0$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186069459 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y}=2x\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069459 1145 "Rearrange to $y'=(2x+1)\\sqrt y$. Since the real-valued problem assumes $y\\ne0$, we have $y>0$, so\n$$\\frac{dy}{\\sqrt y}=(2x+1)\\,dx,\\qquad 2\\sqrt y=x^2+x+C.$$\nThus\n$$y=\\left(\\frac{x^2+x+C}{2}\\right)^2.$$\nUse this formula only on intervals where $x^2+x+C>0$, as required by the unsquared equation." 13194139534556 false] [17592186069459 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534556 true] [17592186069460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534556 false] [17592186069460 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=0$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186069460 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} - (\\cos x)y = 0$ for $y \\ne 0$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186069460 1145 "Rewrite the equation as $y'=y\\cos x$. For $y\\ne0$,\n$$\\frac{dy}{y}=\\cos x\\,dx,\\qquad \\ln|y|=\\sin x+C.$$\nTherefore $y=Ke^{\\sin x}$, where $K\\ne0$." 13194139534556 false] [17592186069460 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295189 13194139534556 true] [17592186295188 1089 "q-14246" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534556 true] [17592186295188 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295188 1113 #uuid "84c2841a-a505-5fa1-920b-ad0e7026b757" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295188 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={e}^{x}+C\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295190 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295191 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295192 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295193 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295194 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1041 "selection" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295194 13194139534556 true] [17592186295189 1110 #uuid "3b1bce90-263b-5083-b9f4-8c401c598651" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295190 1034 "{e}^{y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534556 true] [17592186295190 1111 #uuid "5f52c8c9-d6de-58ee-91ad-bf2e045bc556" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295191 1034 "y={e}^{\\sqrt{x}}+C" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534556 true] [17592186295191 1111 #uuid "3a0a4e59-6e8c-5a15-92e2-ba0b4bf52e8c" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295192 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={e}^{\\sqrt{x}}+C" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534556 true] [17592186295192 1111 #uuid "765f9dac-a050-545d-b2f3-748ddfdc0ec2" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295193 1034 "y={e}^{x}+C" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534556 true] [17592186295193 1111 #uuid "2d2a1b6e-0641-5341-a621-62a841c6e50c" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295194 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={e}^{x}+C" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534556 true] [17592186295194 1111 #uuid "e36f872f-913a-5704-a503-e10668381559" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295195 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295196 13194139534556 true] [17592186295195 1089 "q-158015" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295195 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534556 true] [17592186295195 1100 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295195 1113 #uuid "e27324f6-57f8-5203-af7c-d98f25ea6d41" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295195 1145 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{3}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{{x}^{3}/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}.\n$$" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295197 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295198 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295199 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295200 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295201 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1041 "selection" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295200 13194139534556 true] [17592186295196 1110 #uuid "11ee4694-619b-5a20-822c-d5351e63b769" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295197 1034 "y={e}^{{x}^{2}/2}+K" 13194139534556 true] [17592186295197 